WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.071
This is an implementation guide from
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:08.337
and it's about one product:
CyberArk Certificate Manager.

4
00:00:08.337 --> 00:00:12.869
The workbook's certificate plan chose it
as the program's certificate lifecycle

5
00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:16.588
platform, so whenever you hear me say
"the platform," that's what I mean.

6
00:00:16.588 --> 00:00:20.330
Its job is simple to say and
hard to do: keep track of every

7
00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:24.887
certificate nobody else is renewing,
and renew it before it expires.

8
00:00:24.887 --> 00:00:28.790
We'll start with what the platform is
and what it's made of, then build it,

9
00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.940
connect it to the certificate
authorities, automate it,

10
00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.500
and finally fit it into the
certificate plan's migration waves.

11
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.930
The slides and notes carry every detail
and source; I'll stick to what matters.

12
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.138
Before we build anything, here's the
idea that frames this whole guide.

13
00:00:49.138 --> 00:00:51.593
The workbook's certificate
plan doesn't use

14
00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:54.049
one certificate authority for everything.

15
00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:58.625
It gives each kind of certificate its
own source and its own way of renewing.

16
00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:01.050
Intune looks after device certificates.

17
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.438
PIV cards have their own card system.

18
00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:07.263
Domain controllers keep renewing
themselves the way they always have.

19
00:01:07.263 --> 00:01:12.492
What's left — servers, VPN and RADIUS,
SQL, internal websites, appliances,

20
00:01:12.492 --> 00:01:14.496
and anything public-facing —

21
00:01:14.496 --> 00:01:19.550
has nobody renewing it today except
a person with a calendar reminder.

22
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.650
That's the gap the platform fills.

23
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.309
It becomes the system of record for every

24
00:01:24.309 --> 00:01:27.800
certificate Intune and
PIV don't already manage.

25
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.862
Two things to settle before you build.

26
00:01:32.862 --> 00:01:34.507
First, where it runs.

27
00:01:34.507 --> 00:01:37.856
There's no FedRAMP package
for CyberArk's cloud edition,

28
00:01:37.856 --> 00:01:42.430
so the program runs the self-hosted
edition inside the agency's own boundary,

29
00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.334
next to the certificate
authorities it manages.

30
00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:49.595
Second, what it runs on: Windows
servers, English-language builds,

31
00:01:49.595 --> 00:01:54.987
all on the same version, with a SQL Server
database that lives on its own server.

32
00:01:54.987 --> 00:01:57.738
Plan for an upgrade
roughly every six months,

33
00:01:57.738 --> 00:02:00.260
because that's CyberArk's release rhythm.

34
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.276
The full requirements are on the slide.

35
00:02:02.276 --> 00:02:06.790
Next, let's open the box and see what
the platform is actually made of.

36
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.893
This short section answers
the question people ask first:

37
00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:15.640
what actually is the platform?

38
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.865
Not a box, and not one server.

39
00:02:17.865 --> 00:02:22.005
It's a set of Windows servers running
the same CyberArk software against

40
00:02:22.005 --> 00:02:26.215
one shared database, with different
jobs switched on in different places.

41
00:02:26.215 --> 00:02:29.537
We'll look at those server roles,
the web tier you sign in to,

42
00:02:29.537 --> 00:02:32.222
the back end that does
the work, the components,

43
00:02:32.222 --> 00:02:36.463
the drivers that reach other systems,
what it all connects to, a reference

44
00:02:36.463 --> 00:02:41.490
layout for two sites, and finally one
certificate's journey from start to finish.

45
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.564
Here's the whole platform on one page.

46
00:02:47.564 --> 00:02:52.573
Every server runs the same software and
talks to the same database; what makes

47
00:02:52.573 --> 00:02:57.346
a server a "web server" or a "back-end
server" is which components you switch on.

48
00:02:57.346 --> 00:03:02.443
The web servers sit behind a load balancer
and give people and automation a way in.

49
00:03:02.443 --> 00:03:04.841
The ACME servers are kept separate,

50
00:03:04.841 --> 00:03:09.656
because CyberArk doesn't allow FIPS
mode on a server that handles ACME.

51
00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:11.963
The back-end servers do the real work —

52
00:03:11.963 --> 00:03:15.025
issuing, renewing and
installing certificates.

53
00:03:15.025 --> 00:03:18.891
The log servers keep the audit
trail and send events to the SIEM.

54
00:03:18.891 --> 00:03:23.313
And underneath: one SQL Server
database, the keys that protect it,

55
00:03:23.313 --> 00:03:26.840
and a message bus that
keeps every server in step.

56
00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.540
The web tier is how anyone gets in.

57
00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:36.263
People use the web console for
daily work and the Policy Tree,

58
00:03:36.263 --> 00:03:39.540
which is also a web page,
for the deeper configuration.

59
00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.452
Automation uses the REST API,

60
00:03:41.452 --> 00:03:46.152
and sign-ins for both go through the
platform's authentication service.

61
00:03:46.152 --> 00:03:49.309
There's one more console,
the Configuration Console,

62
00:03:49.309 --> 00:03:53.640
that administrators run on each server
to switch components on and off.

63
00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.006
The thing to remember for the
build is the load balancer:

64
00:03:57.006 --> 00:04:00.883
every web service sits behind one
name, and that name has to match

65
00:04:00.883 --> 00:04:04.970
the certificate the servers
present, or single sign-on breaks.

66
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.020
Behind the scenes, three things matter.

67
00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.454
Processing engines decide which server
does which work, so you can keep, say,

68
00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:20.032
public certificate requests on one server
that's allowed out to the internet.

69
00:04:20.032 --> 00:04:23.041
The log servers record
every event and are the

70
00:04:23.041 --> 00:04:26.208
only servers that need a
path to the SIEM and email.

71
00:04:26.208 --> 00:04:30.541
And the message bus keeps every
server informed about changes,

72
00:04:30.541 --> 00:04:33.458
which means one firewall rule people miss:

73
00:04:33.458 --> 00:04:37.708
port 8883 has to be open between
every server, across sites too.

74
00:04:37.708 --> 00:04:42.080
Watch distance as well — every server
works against one active database,

75
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.320
and at a thirty-millisecond delay a single
renewal takes about fifteen seconds.

76
00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.382
This table is the platform's parts list.

77
00:04:53.382 --> 00:04:56.421
Each component is a job
you switch on per server:

78
00:04:56.421 --> 00:04:59.303
the lifecycle engine
that issues and renews,

79
00:04:59.303 --> 00:05:02.653
CA Import that pulls in what
your CAs already issued,

80
00:05:02.653 --> 00:05:05.926
discovery that finds
certificates on the network,

81
00:05:05.926 --> 00:05:11.613
validation and revocation monitoring that
keep checking, and the enrollment services

82
00:05:11.613 --> 00:05:16.132
— ACME, EST and SCEP — that devices and
scripts use to ask for certificates.

83
00:05:16.132 --> 00:05:18.133
You don't need to memorize the list.

84
00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:20.481
The point is that the design is modular,

85
00:05:20.481 --> 00:05:25.230
so you place each job where its network
access and its security rules fit.

86
00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.046
Drivers are how the platform
reaches the outside world.

87
00:05:32.046 --> 00:05:37.794
CA drivers request certificates
from AD CS, public CAs or AWS.

88
00:05:37.794 --> 00:05:41.966
Application drivers install
certificates on IIS, F5,

89
00:05:41.966 --> 00:05:46.598
NetScaler, Key Vault and the
rest, usually without any agent.

90
00:05:46.598 --> 00:05:48.152
Where a driver can't reach,

91
00:05:48.152 --> 00:05:51.493
a small Server Agent checks
in and does the work locally.

92
00:05:51.493 --> 00:05:54.817
Everything is stored in
one SQL Server database,

93
00:05:54.817 --> 00:05:57.995
and that database's secrets
are protected by a key —

94
00:05:57.995 --> 00:06:02.335
either a software key you must back
up, or a hardware security module.

95
00:06:02.335 --> 00:06:07.250
Lose that key and you lose the data,
which is why it's treated as Tier 0.

96
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.250
Now the bigger picture:
everything the platform touches.

97
00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:18.087
On the left, the certificate
authorities it asks for certificates.

98
00:06:18.087 --> 00:06:20.888
On the right, the systems
it installs them on.

99
00:06:20.888 --> 00:06:23.736
Above, Entra ID for
signing in to the console,

100
00:06:23.736 --> 00:06:27.613
and CyberArk's own vault for
the passwords its drivers need.

101
00:06:27.613 --> 00:06:31.838
Below, the SIEM for events and
ServiceNow for owners and tickets.

102
00:06:31.838 --> 00:06:37.087
Two lines are dashed on purpose: Kubernetes
and ServiceNow both need extra care,

103
00:06:37.087 --> 00:06:40.189
because one depends on
a cloud service outside

104
00:06:40.189 --> 00:06:43.212
the boundary and the other
is a third-party app.

105
00:06:43.212 --> 00:06:44.900
We'll come back to both.

106
00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:51.813
Here's how those pieces could
be laid out across two sites.

107
00:06:51.813 --> 00:06:53.646
This is the program's design,

108
00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:58.112
built only from CyberArk's documented
rules, not a vendor blueprint.

109
00:06:58.112 --> 00:07:01.940
Each site has a web server,
a separate ACME server,

110
00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.524
a back-end server and a log server.

111
00:07:04.524 --> 00:07:08.779
One database runs in an availability
group across both sites,

112
00:07:08.779 --> 00:07:13.450
with one listener everyone uses, and
the HSMs are paired the same way.

113
00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:14.613
Two cautions.

114
00:07:14.613 --> 00:07:17.867
CyberArk doesn't say
whether FIPS and non-FIPS

115
00:07:17.867 --> 00:07:21.550
servers can share one cluster,
so get that in writing.

116
00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:23.077
And keep the sites close,

117
00:07:23.077 --> 00:07:27.560
because the remote site pays for
every round trip to the database.

118
00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:33.960
Let's follow one certificate
through all of that.

119
00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.660
First the platform finds it, by scanning
the network or importing from a CA.

120
00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:42.073
Then it gives it a home in a
policy folder and a named owner.

121
00:07:42.073 --> 00:07:45.862
When it's time, the lifecycle
engine asks the CA for a new one,

122
00:07:45.862 --> 00:07:48.309
and a driver installs it on the server.

123
00:07:48.309 --> 00:07:52.035
Validation keeps checking that it's
really there and still trusted.

124
00:07:52.035 --> 00:07:56.585
Before it expires, the cycle repeats
— no one has to remember a date.

125
00:07:56.585 --> 00:08:00.960
And when the system it belongs to is
retired, the certificate is revoked.

126
00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.772
Every step leaves a log entry, and
that trail is what an assessor reads.

127
00:08:05.772 --> 00:08:08.500
With that picture in mind, let's build it.

128
00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:13.562
Part 1 is the build.

129
00:08:13.562 --> 00:08:16.694
We'll size the cluster and
make it highly available,

130
00:08:16.694 --> 00:08:21.167
be careful about what we claim for
FIPS, set up how administrators sign in,

131
00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:24.140
harden it, and make sure
it can be recovered.

132
00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.040
Sizing is refreshingly simple.

133
00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:33.627
Two servers handle up to about
fifty thousand certificates.

134
00:08:33.627 --> 00:08:38.508
Past that, CyberArk's guide splits the
work across six — two each for web, back

135
00:08:38.508 --> 00:08:43.789
end and logging — and beyond a million
certificates CyberArk designs it with you.

136
00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:48.614
For availability, the rules are what you'd
expect: at least two servers per role,

137
00:08:48.614 --> 00:08:51.327
a load balancer in front
of the web services,

138
00:08:51.327 --> 00:08:54.040
and an availability
group for the database.

139
00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:58.040
One nice touch for the certificate
authorities: a CA pool lets the

140
00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:02.390
platform fail over to a second CA
if the first one stops answering.

141
00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:10.365
FIPS is where it's easy to
overclaim, so let's be precise.

142
00:09:10.365 --> 00:09:14.344
The platform runs on Windows
servers that can be in FIPS mode,

143
00:09:14.344 --> 00:09:15.777
and that's documented.

144
00:09:15.777 --> 00:09:19.869
What isn't documented is a FIPS
certificate for the platform itself —

145
00:09:19.869 --> 00:09:23.303
none of CyberArk's validated
modules names this product.

146
00:09:23.303 --> 00:09:26.452
So the honest evidence
is FIPS-mode Windows,

147
00:09:26.452 --> 00:09:30.477
plus the HSM's own certificate
for the database secrets.

148
00:09:30.477 --> 00:09:34.097
And remember the catch from
earlier: the ACME servers can't

149
00:09:34.097 --> 00:09:37.850
be in FIPS mode at all, which
is why they're kept separate.

150
00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:44.125
Next, how administrators sign in.

151
00:09:44.125 --> 00:09:47.073
The platform supports single
sign-on with Entra ID,

152
00:09:47.073 --> 00:09:50.934
so the program's approach is to put
the console behind Entra and let

153
00:09:50.934 --> 00:09:55.988
administrators use their PIV card through
Entra's certificate-based authentication.

154
00:09:55.988 --> 00:10:00.328
That way there's one trust store and
one set of Conditional Access rules,

155
00:10:00.328 --> 00:10:03.050
instead of a second
login system to maintain.

156
00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:06.500
Keep the platform's own
certificate login as a fallback,

157
00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:10.025
keep a local break-glass
account, and shorten the default

158
00:10:10.025 --> 00:10:13.840
token lifetimes for automation
— ninety days is too long.

159
00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:20.570
Hardening comes down to
one uncomfortable fact:

160
00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:23.028
this platform concentrates privilege.

161
00:10:23.028 --> 00:10:27.456
It holds rights on your certificate
authorities, administrator access on

162
00:10:27.456 --> 00:10:31.814
the servers it installs certificates
on, and private keys in its database.

163
00:10:31.814 --> 00:10:34.308
So apply CyberArk's web server hardening,

164
00:10:34.308 --> 00:10:38.047
replace the self-signed certificate
every server ships with,

165
00:10:38.047 --> 00:10:42.519
switch outbound certificate checking
from its default of "none" to strict,

166
00:10:42.519 --> 00:10:45.452
and keep driver passwords
in the CyberArk vault.

167
00:10:45.452 --> 00:10:50.460
Then treat the admins, the key and
the database backups as Tier 0.

168
00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:58.567
Recovery hinges on a pair: the
database and the key that protects it.

169
00:10:58.567 --> 00:11:02.825
Back up both, store them separately,
and if an HSM holds the key,

170
00:11:02.825 --> 00:11:08.108
run the HSMs as a pair, because the platform
shuts down when it can't reach one.

171
00:11:08.108 --> 00:11:12.177
CyberArk's recovery steps are on
the slide; the one people forget is

172
00:11:12.177 --> 00:11:17.265
re-enabling SQL Service Broker after a
restore, which quietly stops everything.

173
00:11:17.265 --> 00:11:19.592
The program's rule is to rehearse a full

174
00:11:19.592 --> 00:11:22.430
restore before the second
migration wave ends.

175
00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:23.454
That's the build.

176
00:11:23.454 --> 00:11:26.430
Now let's connect it to the
certificate authorities.

177
00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:35.668
Part 2 connects the platform to the places
certificates come from: the agency's own

178
00:11:35.668 --> 00:11:41.520
AD CS, public certificate authorities, the
cloud, and how it lives alongside Intune.

179
00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:50.932
The most important connection is to AD CS,
the agency's own certificate authority.

180
00:11:50.932 --> 00:11:53.020
The platform talks to it over DCOM,

181
00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:57.045
using an account with the right
to issue and manage certificates.

182
00:11:57.045 --> 00:12:00.987
The cleanest pattern is one
platform template for each

183
00:12:00.987 --> 00:12:04.758
AD CS template it issues from,
so policy is easy to read.

184
00:12:04.758 --> 00:12:09.049
After Active Directory retires, the
workbook's certificate plan moves

185
00:12:09.049 --> 00:12:13.558
server certificates to a standalone
CA, and the platform handles that fine.

186
00:12:13.558 --> 00:12:18.818
One open item: CyberArk's support
table stops at Windows Server 2022,

187
00:12:18.818 --> 00:12:23.220
so if your CA runs 2025, get
that confirmed in writing.

188
00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.058
This slide is about one
dangerous permission.

189
00:12:29.058 --> 00:12:31.616
To issue certificates on the agency's CAs,

190
00:12:31.616 --> 00:12:35.680
the platform needs a right called
Issue and Manage Certificates,

191
00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.594
and with it an account can approve,
deny or revoke certificates.

192
00:12:39.594 --> 00:12:43.832
Attackers know this; it's a
known attack path called ESC7.

193
00:12:43.832 --> 00:12:47.376
Here's the honest part: Microsoft
doesn't give you a switch that

194
00:12:47.376 --> 00:12:50.920
limits that right to a few templates,
so we don't pretend it does.

195
00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.555
We limit what the account can
request, prove in the lab that a

196
00:12:54.555 --> 00:12:59.210
request outside those templates fails,
and alert if one ever succeeds.

197
00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:07.399
For public certificates, the platform
has drivers for the major public CAs,

198
00:13:07.399 --> 00:13:10.835
and two changes in this release
matter before you upgrade.

199
00:13:10.835 --> 00:13:13.860
Entrust is no longer
supported as a public CA,

200
00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:16.885
so if that's your provider,
plan the path first.

201
00:13:16.885 --> 00:13:22.098
And DigiCert client certificates now
need an email address, or renewals fail.

202
00:13:22.098 --> 00:13:24.821
On the cloud side, the platform can issue

203
00:13:24.821 --> 00:13:27.873
through AWS and install
into Azure Key Vault.

204
00:13:27.873 --> 00:13:31.799
What I couldn't confirm is Key
Vault support in Azure Government,

205
00:13:31.799 --> 00:13:34.390
so test it or ask before you rely on it.

206
00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:42.227
The rule with Intune is simple:
Intune owns device certificates,

207
00:13:42.227 --> 00:13:44.540
and the platform stays out of its way.

208
00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:48.969
If some Intune certificates still come
from AD CS during the transition,

209
00:13:48.969 --> 00:13:53.028
the platform will see them, and it
just watches — it doesn't renew them.

210
00:13:53.028 --> 00:13:57.648
CyberArk can also act as Intune's certificate
authority, but the program says no,

211
00:13:57.648 --> 00:14:02.128
because that would create a second issuer
for devices that are mid-migration.

212
00:14:02.128 --> 00:14:06.094
And there's no built-in way to
pull in Cloud PKI's inventory;

213
00:14:06.094 --> 00:14:10.930
it would take a custom job, so
Intune's own report stays the record.

214
00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:19.293
Part 3 is where the platform earns
its keep: finding every certificate,

215
00:14:19.293 --> 00:14:24.910
giving each one an owner, and automating
renewal so nobody has to remember a date.

216
00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:30.761
You can't manage what you can't see,

217
00:14:30.761 --> 00:14:33.873
and a CA's database only
shows what that CA issued.

218
00:14:33.873 --> 00:14:37.986
So the platform finds certificates
two ways: it scans the network for

219
00:14:37.986 --> 00:14:42.748
anything presenting a certificate, and
it imports what your CAs already issued.

220
00:14:42.748 --> 00:14:45.672
Agents and API feeds fill in the rest.

221
00:14:45.672 --> 00:14:47.529
The program's rule is blunt:

222
00:14:47.529 --> 00:14:51.834
any certificate found with no
record and no owner is a finding.

223
00:14:51.834 --> 00:14:57.610
In the first wave, run read-only scans
and imports, and log every surprise.

224
00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:03.938
An expired certificate is almost always an

225
00:15:03.938 --> 00:15:06.660
ownership problem before
it's a technical one.

226
00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.835
So every certificate gets
a home and a named owner.

227
00:15:09.835 --> 00:15:14.226
The platform's policy folders mirror
the workbook's certificate classes,

228
00:15:14.226 --> 00:15:17.322
so where a certificate lives
tells you how it renews.

229
00:15:17.322 --> 00:15:20.976
Owners come from Entra groups,
and, if the agency uses it,

230
00:15:20.976 --> 00:15:26.660
the ServiceNow app can tie certificates to
the same configuration items as the CMDB.

231
00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:30.725
And one alert matters most: a
certificate nearing expiry with no

232
00:15:30.725 --> 00:15:34.920
renewal path is an incident while
there's still time to fix it.

233
00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:42.172
Automation is how renewals
stop depending on people.

234
00:15:42.172 --> 00:15:45.110
Web servers can renew
themselves using ACME,

235
00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:48.285
the same protocol public
certificate sites use.

236
00:15:48.285 --> 00:15:51.835
Network devices can use EST or SCEP.

237
00:15:51.835 --> 00:15:56.735
Scripts and pipelines use the REST
API or CyberArk's command-line tool.

238
00:15:56.735 --> 00:16:01.323
And for everything else, the drivers renew
the certificate and install it for you.

239
00:16:01.323 --> 00:16:05.907
The one design rule to carry
forward is the one we've seen twice:

240
00:16:05.907 --> 00:16:09.690
ACME runs on its own
servers, outside FIPS mode.

241
00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.590
Three integrations, each
with a boundary question.

242
00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:20.600
Kubernetes discovery only works
through CyberArk's cloud service,

243
00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:24.466
which isn't authorized, so inside
the boundary we use cert-manager,

244
00:16:24.466 --> 00:16:27.402
which talks straight to
the self-hosted platform.

245
00:16:27.402 --> 00:16:31.355
ServiceNow has no CyberArk-built
app, only a third-party one,

246
00:16:31.355 --> 00:16:34.340
so assess it like any
supply-chain component.

247
00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:39.107
And the SIEM gets the platform's events
over syslog with TLS — with one gotcha:

248
00:16:39.107 --> 00:16:42.859
the SIEM collector's certificate
needs its IP address in it,

249
00:16:42.859 --> 00:16:44.940
even if you connect by name.

250
00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:51.917
Part 4 puts it all on a
timeline and keeps it running:

251
00:16:51.917 --> 00:16:55.221
the build mapped to the
certificate plan's six waves,

252
00:16:55.221 --> 00:17:00.580
renewal as certificate lifetimes shrink,
monitoring, and retiring the old CAs.

253
00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:08.950
Here's the build laid onto the workbook's
certificate plan and its six waves.

254
00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:12.494
Wave one is a small test
cluster and read-only discovery,

255
00:17:12.494 --> 00:17:14.112
so you learn what you have.

256
00:17:14.112 --> 00:17:18.642
Wave two builds production properly
— two of everything, keys backed up,

257
00:17:18.642 --> 00:17:23.562
sign-in and logging done — and doesn't
close until a restore has been rehearsed.

258
00:17:23.562 --> 00:17:27.773
Wave three pilots automatic
renewal on a handful of servers,

259
00:17:27.773 --> 00:17:30.637
with the old method left
on as a safety net.

260
00:17:30.637 --> 00:17:34.478
Then you scale ring by ring,
move public certificates over,

261
00:17:34.478 --> 00:17:38.990
and finally retire CAs using the
platform's inventory as proof.

262
00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:43.148
Here's why this can't wait.

263
00:17:43.148 --> 00:17:48.303
Public certificate lifetimes are
shrinking fast: two hundred days today,

264
00:17:48.303 --> 00:17:51.598
a hundred next year, and
forty-seven days by 2029.

265
00:17:51.598 --> 00:17:55.102
For a thousand certificates,
that's roughly seven

266
00:17:55.102 --> 00:17:58.948
renewals every working day
now, and over thirty in 2029.

267
00:17:58.948 --> 00:18:00.948
No team can keep that up by hand.

268
00:18:00.948 --> 00:18:05.800
That's why the workbook's certificate plan
rules that no certificate may depend on a

269
00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:09.104
person remembering a date —
every one runs through Intune,

270
00:18:09.104 --> 00:18:11.430
autoenrollment, or this platform.

271
00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:17.960
Monitoring is where
the inventory pays off.

272
00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:22.457
The platform sends its events to the SIEM
and watches for four kinds of drift:

273
00:18:22.457 --> 00:18:25.575
a certificate close to expiry
with no way to renew it,

274
00:18:25.575 --> 00:18:29.999
a certificate issued outside policy,
a revocation list that's going stale,

275
00:18:29.999 --> 00:18:33.698
and trust stores that have picked
up roots they shouldn't have.

276
00:18:33.698 --> 00:18:38.024
The checks run at least weekly, and the
results are kept, because an assessor

277
00:18:38.024 --> 00:18:42.430
will ask how long a problem stayed
open, not just whether it's open today.

278
00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:49.252
Eventually the old certificate
authorities retire,

279
00:18:49.252 --> 00:18:51.905
and the platform is the
evidence that it's safe.

280
00:18:51.905 --> 00:18:56.717
Before a CA goes, its inventory has to
show that nothing still depends on it.

281
00:18:56.717 --> 00:19:01.038
Then you stop new issuance — after
pointing the platform's templates at a

282
00:19:01.038 --> 00:19:05.505
CA that's staying — publish the last
revocation list, and remove the service.

283
00:19:05.505 --> 00:19:09.768
The records and endpoints stay up
until the last certificate expires.

284
00:19:09.768 --> 00:19:13.256
After Active Directory, server
certificates come from a

285
00:19:13.256 --> 00:19:17.490
standalone CA under the same root,
still driven by the platform.

286
00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:22.115
To wrap up.

287
00:19:22.115 --> 00:19:26.267
The decisions are yours: which
servers run in FIPS mode,

288
00:19:26.267 --> 00:19:30.603
HSM or software key, and a
handful of integration choices.

289
00:19:30.603 --> 00:19:34.440
There are a few questions to
put to CyberArk in writing,

290
00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:38.790
mainly around FIPS, Windows Server
2025, and Azure Government.

291
00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:43.873
And the next steps are concrete: stand up
the test cluster, create the CA account

292
00:19:43.873 --> 00:19:48.878
under the ESC7 controls, and prove in the
lab that it can't do more than it should.

293
00:19:48.878 --> 00:19:52.457
The program's view is clear — this
is the right platform for the

294
00:19:52.457 --> 00:19:56.466
workbook's certificate plan, and
its risks are known and manageable.

295
00:19:56.466 --> 00:19:59.356
This is the program's
research, under review,

296
00:19:59.356 --> 00:20:02.327
and not an endorsement
by any federal agency.

297
00:20:02.327 --> 00:20:03.480
Thanks for watching.
